/zipball/v0.1.0","body":"# _aria_ — first public release\r\n\r\n**A dependency-free C/CUDA runtime for Stable Audio 3.** Text-to-music generation that runs on a commodity GPU, a CPU-only box, or a Raspberry Pi 5 — no Python, no PyTorch, no framework underneath.\r\n\r\nThis is the first public release of *aria*. It runs the complete Stable Audio 3 pipeline (tokenizer, T5Gemma text encoder, diffusion-transformer denoiser, and audio autoencoder) as a single native binary, with no dependencies beyond a C math library and OpenMP.\r\n\r\n## Why\r\n\r\nOpen music models are good enough to build with, but their reference stacks assume a Python serving layer and a discrete GPU. That's a poor fit for local tools, edge devices, and anything that has to start fast or stay resident near the user. aria removes the serving stack and asks how much of the deployment cost was ever the model itself.\r\n\r\n## Quick start\r\n\r\n```sh\r\n# 1. Build\r\nmake                          # CPU-only\r\nmake cuda CUDA_ARCH=sm_86     # + GPU (set your arch)\r\n\r\n# 2. Fetch weights — Stable Audio 3 is gated: accept the license on the model page, then log in\r\n./download_model.sh small-music\r\n\r\n# 3. Generate\r\n./aria -m models/small-music -p \"warm ambient piano, slow, consonant\" -d 10 --seed 0 -o out.wav\r\n```\r\n\r\n`./aria --help` covers the rest: continue / inpaint, streaming, batch, LoRA, and benchmarking.\r\n\r\nIssues and feedback welcome. See `LICENSE` for the runtime's terms; the Stable Audio 3 weights are\r\ngoverned by Stability AI's model license.\r\n"}]